+`validate` sits outside that rule on purpose: it is a client of a running deployment, not a part of one, and depends on no `wyrd-*` crate (proposal 0017 §9). Its command-line surface is ten required flags — `--endpoint`, `--region`, `--bucket`, `--scenario`, `--duration`, `--workers`, `--seed`, `--out`, `--run-id`, `--driver-placement` — with S3 credentials taken from `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`/`AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, else `WYRD_S3_ACCESS_KEY`/`WYRD_S3_SECRET_KEY`, else refused; half a pair, or a variable whose value is not UTF-8, is refused by name rather than skipped over to the next pair (an empty variable counts as unset). Parsing is strict, unlike `wyrd`'s: an unknown flag, a repeated flag, an empty value, a stray positional, or a `--`-prefixed token in a value slot is refused by name rather than absorbed. The library carries the S3 client every scenario calls through (#852): `aws-sdk-s3` on the `--endpoint`, path-style, with static SigV4 credentials, plain HTTP (TLS is terminated in front of the gateway), and retries and stalled-stream protection off. Its error is typed: HTTP status, `<Code>`, `<Message>`, and the `x-amz-request-id` read from that header itself. Connect, operation, and body-idle deadlines bound every wait, and object bodies stream both ways, so what the client holds of an object does not grow with its size. The AWS SDK is the only shipped dependency `validate` adds; Wyrd crates appear only as dev-dependencies, for the in-process gateway its tests run against. The binary still echoes its resolved configuration (the access-key id, never the secret) and exits without issuing a request; scenarios arrive with #743.
